\details{
\strong{Sample and features.} The 620 participants who reported gender (as
in Pargent et al.), and all 1,821 behavioral features aggregated over
several weeks of smartphone logging. Features with missing values are
median-imputed, the median taken once over the 620 participants; this uses
the covariates only, never the outcome, so the design quantities remain
design-stage quantities.

\strong{Designs.} The predictor sets of \code{p} = 25, 100 and 600 are the
first \code{p} features of one random ordering of the features, and
\code{p} = 1821 is the full set. The ordering is random by design: the
object characterises what designs of this size and leverage structure
deliver, and is not a selected subset. Selecting predictors on the outcome
would invalidate the in-sample R-squared as an estimate of the population
signal. Each design appears on the raw scale and after a rank-normal
transformation of every column (\code{(rank - 0.5)/n} mapped through
\code{qnorm}); a transformed predictor is a different predictor, so each
scale has its own signal estimate.

\strong{Derived quantities only.} Leverage, \eqn{D^*},
\eqn{D^*(\kappa^*)}, \eqn{p_{eff}} and the break-even values are computed
from the covariate matrix with no outcome involved. The in-sample R-squared,
the estimate and interval for \eqn{\rho^2_{in}}, and the realized
leave-one-out values use the outcome. The features themselves are not
redistributed -- see the source.
}
